    {
      "id": "f5a600b24782079b65fae0dad1ea946a7d7a7af3",
      "tag": "autogenerated:ai-review",
      "author": {
        "_account_id": 1345,
        "name": "Gerrit AI review for Lustre",
        "display_name": "AI review",
        "email": "gaireview@linuxhacker.ru",
        "username": "aireview",
        "status": "Contact Oleg Drokin (green@whamcloud.com) with any questions on this account"
      },
      "date": "2026-08-11 08:51:29.000000000",
      "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\n(1 comment)\n\nAI code review results below, please doublecheck\n\nMostly good. The removal itself checks out: the four deleted patches were referenced only by 3.10-rhel7.9.series, and `contrib/scripts/clearpatches.sh lustre/kernel_patches` comes back clean afterwards. The ldiskfs case being dropped was already dangling since the ldiskfs-3.10-rhel7.9.series file went away in de607f1962.\n\nThe cleanup looks incomplete though. Removing contrib/lbuild/lbuild-rhel7 orphans contrib/lbuild/rhel7/ (kmodtool.patch, rpmmacros.template, .gitignore) — that script was the only consumer, and nothing else in the tree references the directory. Since el7 is being retired here, would it make sense to drop the rest in the same patch: lustre/kernel_patches/targets/3.10-rhel7.9.target.in (its SERIES= line now points at the file this patch deletes) and its config/lustre-core.m4 entry, lustre/kernel_patches/kernel_configs/kernel-3.10.0-3.10-rhel7.9-x86_64.config (only referenced by the patch_spec() being deleted), and the rhel7 cases in contrib/lbuild/funcs.sh and contrib/lbuild/lbuild that now map to a handler that no longer exists?",
      "accounts_in_message": [],
      "_revision_number": 1
    }
